os","orcid":"0009-0002-4687-796X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/13548565251332744","issn":["13548565","17487382"],"issn_org":["13548565"],"issue":"4","journal":"Convergence: The International Journal of Research into New Media Technologies","pages":"1506-1508","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"Convergence: The International Journal of Research into New Media Technologies","volume":"31"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-fc472255-d008-4c37-92f1-8431583bdc93"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Book review: Algorithms of resistance Bonini T and Treré E (2024)  Algorithms of resistance: The everyday fight against platform power . Cambridge, MA: The MIT Press. 256 pp. ISBN 9780262547420 (paperback); ISBN 9780262377492 (ebook)","type":"Journal Review","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818258","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Algorithmic management has been a prominent focus of platform labour studies and is lately receiving increasing academic and policymaking interest. Conceptualisations of algorithmic management are mainly premised on ridehailing, crowdwork and food delivery platform research, while literature on algorithmic management of housecleaning and domestic work platforms is limited. This article draws on research in housecleaning platform labour in Denmark, demonstrating how algorithmic management unfolds in these platforms, which are the institutional devices which support algorithms in practice, and how platform workers experience, make sense of and resist this type of management. The analysis of the findings highlights two factors that greatly influence the algorithmic management process. The first is the role of customer support departments in underpinning – and sometimes substituting – the functions widely considered to be carried out by algorithms. The second factor is that intersectional subjectivities of – predominantly migrant and female – workers of Danish housecleaning platforms affect the ways in which workers experience and respond to their algorithmic management. The article concludes in proposing minor algorithmic management, as a concept more fitting to simultaneously describe the entanglement of human, social, and algorithmic components comprising housecleaning platforms’ algorithmic management and the contingent nature of migrant platform housecleaners’ compliance with such management.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Floros, Konstantinos"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776444"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Algorithmic management","Denmark","Gig work","Housecleaning Platforms"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"rethinking algorithmic management in minor key the case of housecleaning platform labour in denmark","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/29768624241273468","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776444","ids":["itu-b706fa5f-7bf1-4548-b68a-cfde91d05771"],"keyword":["Algorithmic Management","Denmark","gig work","housecleaning platforms","minor theory"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-AD14A351796A120E87AD11F0DBA1E8A7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"rem","url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/reader/10.1177/29768624241273468","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/29768624241273468","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Konstantinos","key":"florospau","last":"Floros","locid":"20ad2ae0-df32-484d-84ed-f65810600cfa","name":"Floros, Konstantinos","orcid":"0009-0002-4687-796X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"]},"sid":["itu-b706fa5f-7bf1-4548-b68a-cfde91d05771"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Rethinking algorithmic management in minor key: The case of housecleaning platform labour in Denmark","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776444","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"This article problematises the invocation of the stepping stone hypothesis for platform labour by Danish policymakers, namely the argument that digital labour platforms facilitate transitioning to typical employment, especially for groups of vulnerable workers. Tracing the origins of the stepping stone hypothesis back to the international literature on temporary work agencies and workfare policies triggered by neoliberal policy shifts, the article explores the ideological underpinning of the hypothesis in the Danish context, as Danish policymakers invoked it without possessing relevant data. This article questions the stepping stone narrative drawing on policy document analysis and expert interviews, but also on interviews with platform housecleaners, whose backgrounds, experiences, and aspirations indicate that they do not perceive platform labour as a stepping stone. Early Danish research on platform labour has not been premised on the workers’ perspectives. We claim that policymakers’ positive depiction of platform labour serves towards social and institutional stabilisation of technoprecarious – migrant – labour as a desired and inescapable future of work within the Danish labour market.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Galis, Vasilis","Floros, Konstantinos"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776418"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Denmark","Gig workers","Policymaking","Stepping stone","migrant labour","platform labour"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"platform labour as a stepping stone challenging the dominant narrative for danish housecleaning platforms","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-776418","identifier":[{"id":"85211124678","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-7ca83646-d8a7-438b-bf3b-066d4df3e648"],"keyword":["Denmark","gig workers","migrant labour","platform labour","policymaking","stepping stone","technoprecarity;"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-D3A0F386154CCA19481D1E363C939703"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasilis","key":"galispau","last":"Galis","locid":"443362d7-b255-460d-a559-0fa80a8d9fbc","name":"Galis, Vasilis","orcid":"0000-0002-4621-3699","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Konstantinos","key":"florospau","last":"Floros","locid":"20ad2ae0-df32-484d-84ed-f65810600cfa","name":"Floros, Konstantinos","orcid":"0009-0002-4687-796X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"X"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Undefined"]},"sid":["itu-7ca83646-d8a7-438b-bf3b-066d4df3e648"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Platform Labour as a Stepping Stone? : Challenging the Dominant Narrative for Danish Housecleaning Platforms","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776418","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Digital labor platforms proliferated all over the world in the last fifteen years creating the gig economy. The growth of the gig economy sparked international debates on social, regulatory, and academic level, in relation to (mis)classification of employment relations and algorithmic management of workers. Especially regarding location-based platforms, emerging literature demonstrates and describes the prevalence of migrants in their labor force and the implications stemming thereof. The Danish gig-economy is a remarkable case within this global setting, due to several particularities of the Danish social, political and economic environment. These include the traditional regulation of the labor market through collective agreements between the social partners, and the existence of a universal welfare state coupled with restrictive migration policies. This thesis is a case study engaging with the phenomenon of housecleaning platform labor in Denmark, analyzing it as an outcome simultaneously shaped by three different factors: a) workers’ livelihood strategies, b) platforms’ affordances and performative aspects, and c) policymaking and regulations on labor market, welfare and migration issues. Research on domestic work and housecleaning platforms has been underdeveloped in comparison to other platforms, also because of the invisibility of platform housecleaners in the public sphere, which creates practical and methodological obstacles in conducting fieldwork. Thus, this thesis addresses the relative absence of platform housecleaners’ experiences and practices in international literature and brings the cleaners’ voices to the forefront of debates on platform work in Denmark. This dissertation draws on interviews with – predominantly female and migrant – platform housecleaners, stakeholders, digital ethnography in housecleaners’ social media, policy document analysis and other forms of desk-based research. Platform housecleaning is a precarious form of work, which unfolds on the basis of a double – often unfulfilled – promise. Platform companies promise unhindered flexibility to cleaners and platform workers promise themselves that this work will only be a temporary engagement. Delving deeper into workers’ practices, in the thesis I discern the ways in which they resist their exploitation by platform companies and customers, and how they navigate restrictive regulatory frameworks, welfare exclusions and their minor algorithmic management by platforms. Finally, I examine the role of the Danish state and its institutions over time, in promoting platform housecleaning. Despite providing a lifeline for migrants in urgent need of income, platform housecleaning in Denmark is exacerbating labor market inequalities and augmenting insecurities for its workforce.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Floros, Konstantinos"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-747218"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"imperfect presents uncertain futures platform housecleaners in the danish gig economy","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"doi","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109068665/Phd_Thesis_Final_version_Konstantinos_Flores.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-747218","ids":["itu-360a8600-d4a8-4091-9807-8bc1070d245e"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-B65F2AAD163710BA41C4B8AB7D9A0EAC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109068665/Phd_Thesis_Final_version_Konstantinos_Flores.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Konstantinos","key":"florospau","last":"Floros","locid":"20ad2ae0-df32-484d-84ed-f65810600cfa","name":"Floros, Konstantinos","orcid":"0009-0002-4687-796X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788779494169"],"issn":["16023536"],"issue":"220","pages":"265","publisher":"IT-Universitetet","series":"ITU-DS","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-360a8600-d4a8-4091-9807-8bc1070d245e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Imperfect presents, uncertain futures : Platform housecleaners in the Danish gig economy","type":"Other-Internet Publication","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-747218","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"The worldwide expansion of digital labour platforms has a transformative impact on labour markets, reconfiguring employment relations and labour management both on a local and global scale. Lately, the growing literature on digital labour platforms is increasingly documenting how platform workers around the world are to a great extent migrants. Our article draws on data from empirical research on digital platforms providing housecleaning in Denmark, to emphasise how the intersection of migrant work, digital technologies, labour market regulations and migration law exacerbate inequalities and institutionalise precarious working conditions. We analyse platform housecleaning in Denmark through the lens of the “institutionalisation of precarity” and “Autonomy of Migration” concepts, to highlight that it is a phenomenon simultaneously co-constructed by migrants’ agency and structural factors. We conclude that critical studies on platform labour and future research should engage deeper with the intersecting realities (legal, social, gendered etc.) that shape migrant workers’ precarious lives, and migrants’ own strategies to navigate the shortcomings of exclusive and hostile labour market environments.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Floros, Konstantinos","Jørgensen, Martin Bak"],"cuid":["S02-093.60-672225"],"filters":{"added":"2023-07","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Denmark","Gig economy","Housecleaning Platforms","Labour","autonomy of migration","institutionalisation of precarity"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"danish is never a requirement for these jobs platform housecleaning in denmark through a migration lens","src":["aau","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.12893/gjcpi.2022.3.5","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-093.60-672225","ids":["aau-0af31719-58cb-4997-b89b-34af7bd68ad8","itu-e5aaf524-d54a-420c-8c18-754fe7555317"],"keyword":["Denmark","autonomy of migration","gig-economy","housecleaning platforms","institutionalisation of precarity","labour"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9C9764AA0052DFAF4A3CB8DD04F4B97D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/532404405/Open_Access_Article.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://glocalismjournal.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/Floros_Jorgensen_gjcpi_2022_3.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.12893/gjcpi.2022.3.5","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Culture and Learning","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Culture and Learning, Democracy, Migration and Society (DEMOS)","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Politics and Society, MIX - Center for Displacement, Migration and Integration"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Konstantinos","key":"florospau","last":"Floros","locid":"20ad2ae0-df32-484d-84ed-f65810600cfa","name":"Floros, Konstantinos","orcid":"0009-0002-4687-796X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Martin Bak","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"5e41f079-bbd2-4a57-a1d3-8fad8c9233de","name":"Jørgensen, Martin Bak","orcid":"0000-0003-0104-8543","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.12893/gjcpi.2022.3.5","issn":["22837949"],"issue":"3","journal":"GLOCALISM: JOURNAL OF CULTURE, POLITICS AND INNOVATION","pages":"1-28","status":"p","title":"GLOCALISM: JOURNAL OF CULTURE, POLITICS AND INNOVATION","title_org":"Glocalism: Journal of Culture, Politics and Innovation","volume":"2022"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-0af31719-58cb-4997-b89b-34af7bd68ad8","itu-e5aaf524-d54a-420c-8c18-754fe7555317"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"“Danish is never a Requirement for these Jobs”: Platform Housecleaning in Denmark through a Migration Lens","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-093.60-672225","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"This paper is a unique roundtable discussion between geographers to explore, contextualize and problematize the role of geography in the gig economy. It brings together eight researchers from across Europe all working with qualitative methods and studying the gig economy. Based on reflections and commentaries regarding the spatialities and temporalities in and of the gig economy, we offer an innovative approach to exploring complicated factors in an emerging and rapidly growing field. We highlight the multiple layers of geography in physical and digital spaces and the, sometimes blurry, interactions between these. We also show how temporalities shape the geographies of the gig economy. This paper contributes to developing, deepening and advancing theoretical challenges in understanding the gig economy. It also brings these challenges into an accessible, yet thorough publication that can be used in teaching about the gig economy and digital geography. We provide a pedagogical tool to support university teachers in using this document in their courses.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Webster, Natasha A.","Zhang, Qian","Butler, Olivia","Dissing Christensen, Mathilde","Duus, Katrine","Floros, Konstantinos","Kusk, Kalle","Roelofsen, Maartje"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-712532"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Spain","Sweden","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Cardiff University","Open University of Catalonia","Stockholm University","Uppsala University","Örebro University"],"keyword":["Digital mediation","Geography","Gig economy","Inequality","Platform economy","Platform labor"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":[