hagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Humanities, Department of Communication"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kalle","key":"kuskpau","last":"Kusk","locid":"7360505e-aeea-49ea-bbbb-a3aef6d4e6f9","name":"Kusk, Kalle","orcid":"0000-0001-9790-6324","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Katrine","key":"duuspau","last":"Duus","locid":"d8a217a6-2f68-4708-a6df-235303a49436","name":"Terkelsen, Katrine Duus","name_org":"Duus, Katrine","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Sne","key":"scotthansenpau","last":"Scott Hansen","locid":"9bbc0fae-1376-466c-a819-af15860edf83","name":"Scott Hansen, Sne","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Konstantinos","key":"florospau","last":"Floros","locid":"20ad2ae0-df32-484d-84ed-f65810600cfa","name":"Floros, Konstantinos","orcid":"0009-0002-4687-796X","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.7146/tfa.v24i3.134746","issn":["13991442"],"issue":"3","journal":"Tidsskrift for Arbejdsliv","pages":"28-42","publisher":"Werks Grafiske Hus","status":"p","title":"Tidsskrift for Arbejdsliv","volume":"24"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-7774c909-80da-4c82-98d1-0c2bf3744bbb","itu-d6f6fa00-2b43-4f5f-b95c-9851878357e1","ku-a57f2727-d276-474e-8ff0-84e157430495"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Det usynlige menneske i platformsarbejde : En kvalitativ undersøgelse af algoritmisk ledelse","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-091.72-666311","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"The worldwide proliferation of platform mediated work in the past decade -be this remote online or geographically tethered gig-work- has produced a growing body of literature on the gig-economy and the future of work. The digitalization of the workplace and its algorithmic management have been a common research focus for both supporters and critics of this organizational model (Parker et al. 2016, Wood et al. 2019). This paper claims that it is not only the affordances of digital platforms that reshape work due to e.g., the performativity of rating systems, the complexity of opaque algorithms and the “workforce-as-a-service” model (van Doorn 2017). Investigating the employment practices deriving from platform work, the paper draws on empirical fieldwork on housecleaning platforms in Denmark to claim the existence of a trilateral sustainment of apathy towards technology-driven shifts in traditional employment relations. First, platform housecleaners are working without challenging the ills of their employment (lack of pension schemes, holiday pay, entitlement to benefits etc.), apathetically enduring the way the platform economy has been imposed on the labor market. Second, the Danish state and stakeholders have demonstrated an apathetic behavior towards swiftly regulating the platform labor market and aligning it to the Danish business model. Third, customers using platform housecleaning are usually indifferent as to how such low prices are available to them. This paper aspires to empirically trace the generators of this trilateral apathy which sustains underprivileged employment conditions for platform housecleaners and conceptualize apathy (cf. Lertzman 2013) as a driving force for the establishment of the platform business model. Studying platform-related labor transformations through the lens of apathy can enhance the understanding of their derivation by avoiding the pitfall of technological determinism. van Doorn, N. (2017). Platform labor: On the gendered and racialized exploitation of low-income service work in the ‘on-demand’ economy. Information, Communication & Society, 20(6), 898–914. Lertzman, R. A. (2013). The myth of apathy: Psychoanalytic explorations of environmental subjectivity. In Engaging with Climate Change (pp. 139-165). Routledge. Parker, G. G., Van Alstyne, M. W., & Choudary, S. P. (2016). Platform revolution: How networked markets are transforming the economy and how to make them work for you. WW Norton & Company. Wood, A. J., Graham, M., Lehdonvirta, V., & Hjorth, I. (2019). Good Gig, Bad Gig: Autonomy and Algorithmic Control in the Global Gig Economy. Work, Employment and Society, 33(1), 56–75.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Floros, Konstantinos"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381676"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Apathy","Denmark","Platform economy","Platforms","Policymaking"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"when innovation meets apathy transforming work through the platform business model","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-381676","ids":["itu-1b6705ba-bcf3-4d75-b66e-356724aa38c4"],"keyword":["Denmark","apathy","platform economy","platforms","policymaking"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-D507FB3567FB3ECEF4EEE7348766657F"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Konstantinos","key":"florospau","last":"Floros","locid":"20ad2ae0-df32-484d-84ed-f65810600cfa","name":"Floros, Konstantinos","orcid":"0009-0002-4687-796X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-1b6705ba-bcf3-4d75-b66e-356724aa38c4"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"When innovation meets apathy : Transforming work through the platform business model","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381676","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"The inequalities and displacements produced by the proliferation of platform-mediated gig work and algorithmic management have created a growing literature in recent years (e.g., van Doorn et al. 2020; Lee et al. 2015). Particularly important to our work is the fact that platforms operate largely uncontrolled in terms of employment relations and algorithmic transparency (cf. Rosenblat & Stark 2016). To delve deeper into this question in our local context of Denmark we have established the Danish Research Network for Platform Work. The research within this network has so far resulted in 28 interviews with housecleaning and food-delivery platform workers in three locations in Denmark and we have also undertaken platform work ourselves in the period from 2019 and ongoing. A commonality between our projects is our focus on utilizing ethnographic methodologies which we use to go beyond numbers and algorithms to uncover the lived experience of the platform workers. Our preliminary findings point to a range of locally contingent factors that our interlocutors navigate within. Amongst these are working-holiday visas for migrant workers, tax exemptions, vehicle availability and more general labor market particularities. Moreover, the findings point to varied temporalities of workers’ participation, shaped by the interplay between locally contingent factors and our interlocutors’ specific situations. We argue that it is essential to complement our understanding of how platform workers’ livelihoods are shaped on a local level, by investigating which part of the labor force is attracted to this type of work (and why), how it is governed and whether the effect of the platform business model is to produce, sustain or simply take advantage of pre-existing inequalities. We bring these concerns of social justice and inequality as well as methodological implications to the fore in our discussion. van Doorn, N., Ferrari, F., & Graham, M. (2020). Migration and Migrant Labour in the Gig Economy: An Intervention. SSRN Electronic Journal. https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.3622589 Lee, M. K., Kusbit, D., Metsky, E., & Dabbish, L. (2015). Working with machines: The impact of algorithmic and data-driven management on human workers. In Proceedings of the 33rd annual ACM conference on human factors in computing systems (pp. 1603-1612). Rosenblat, A., & Stark, L. (2016). Algorithmic labor and information asymmetries: A case study of Uber’s drivers. International Journal of Communication, 10, 27.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Floros, Konstantinos","Kusk Gjetting, Kalle","Duus Terkelsen, Katrine","Scott Hansen, Sne"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382461"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Denmark","Ethnographic methods","Labour","Platform economy"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"going local ethnographic investigation of spatio temporal factors in the danish context of digital platform labor","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://erepo.uef.fi/bitstream/handle/123456789/27472/urn_isbn_978-952-61-4585-3.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382461","ids":["itu-4fbc2c46-78f7-4f40-89ba-b1d4f549202b"],"keyword":["Denmark","ethnographic methods","labour","platform economy"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-9BDEA4DE65197CDC44FC4909D410D05B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://erepo.uef.fi/bitstream/handle/123456789/27472/urn_isbn_978-952-61-4585-3.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Konstantinos","key":"florospau","last":"Floros","locid":"20ad2ae0-df32-484d-84ed-f65810600cfa","name":"Floros, Konstantinos","orcid":"0009-0002-4687-796X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kalle","key":"kuskgjettingpau","last":"Kusk Gjetting","name":"Kusk Gjetting, Kalle","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Katrine","key":"duusterkelsenpau","last":"Duus Terkelsen","name":"Duus Terkelsen, Katrine","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Sne","key":"scotthansenpau","last":"Scott Hansen","name":"Scott Hansen, Sne","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-4fbc2c46-78f7-4f40-89ba-b1d4f549202b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Going Local : Ethnographic investigation of spatio-temporal factors in the Danish context of digital platform labor","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382461","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"In recent years, there has been great concern that contracting remote or localized work through digital labor platforms will shape the future of work and employment relations (e.g., Ilsøe & Larsen, 2020). Despite a lack of agreement within the literature on whether the platform economy has been growing in steady, fast or exponential ways, it is rather safe to admit that working through platforms is a consolidated work form in the global labor market, challenging traditional full-time, dependent employment. Both the Danish government (Regeringen, 2019) and EU institutions have stressed the need to cover growing demands for flexible employment through platforms which “create jobs and improve competitiveness” (European Commission, 2018). This paper combines digital ethnography, document analysis and interviews with housecleaners and stakeholders to investigate the nexus between flexible and precarious employment in Danish housecleaning platforms. It argues that the composition of the labor force working through these platforms and the everyday practices within platform housecleaning challenge the positive character attributed to the state-supported sociotechnical imaginary of the Danish platform economy and goes further to question whether such an imaginary exists or if it forms part of a broader Danish sociotechnical imaginary of the digitalization of everyday life (cf Jasanoff, 2015). In line with this year’s DASTS theme the paper claims that digital housecleaning platforms build on the affordances inherent to the platform business model (cost-efficient algorithmic management, performativity of ratings, competition etc.) while sustaining “normal” (atypical, low-paid) employment conditions for the highly gendered and racialized workforce of housecleaners in Denmark.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Floros, Konstantinos"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383881"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Denmark","Digitalisation","Platform economy","Sociotechnical imaginaries"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"housecleaning platforms in denmark how does the past fit in the imagined future","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-383881","ids":["itu-b3a97afc-73f6-493a-be31-a6620c23898d"],"keyword":["Denmark","Digitalisation","platform economy","sociotechnical imaginaries"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-FF3E2B8A056C2D754BADC3042BDEEDC0"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Konstantinos","key":"florospau","last":"Floros","locid":"20ad2ae0-df32-484d-84ed-f65810600cfa","name":"Floros, Konstantinos","orcid":"0009-0002-4687-796X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b3a97afc-73f6-493a-be31-a6620c23898d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Housecleaning platforms in Denmark: How does the past fit in the imagined future?","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383881","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Algorithmic management of digital labor platforms and workers’ practices to regain control of their autonomy have produced a growing literature on the “gig economy” in recent years. Despite much attention paid to ridehailing and crowdwork platforms, research work on, the less tech-intensive, housecleaning platforms is limited. The state-supported sociotechnical imaginary (Jasanoff 2015) of the platform economy in Denmark is promoted as offering opportunities to businesses and consumers. Platforms operate largely uncontrolled in terms of employment relations, tax issues, algorithmic transparency, and social dumping conditions. Nevertheless, many workers sign up to them either pursuing -what they term as- more flexible working arrangements or enduring and potentially resisting precarious employment. This paper investigates relations and practices in the Danish housecleaning platform economy, which is characterized by a proliferation of female, young, migrant workers of predominantly Latin-American origin. It engages ethnographically with platform housecleaners aspiring to analyze perceptions that are articulated from below, on both the nature of flexible/precarious working conditions and the role of technology in their consolidation. This study is not limited to platform and app-related features (such as rating, surveillance, algorithm-produced suggestions for customers, profile uploading etc.) but also aims to scrutinize how the Danish digitalized welfare state’s policies and infrastructure potentially contribute to workers’ insecurity. This paper claims that the double evolution towards an individualization of risk for workers and an individualization of social problems promoted by contemporary digital welfare states creates the adequate political ecology for the proliferation of platform work, within an environment of structured uncertainty.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Floros, Konstantinos"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381702"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Algorithmic management","Digital welfare state","Gig economy","Platform labor","Precarious employment"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"fostering flexibility or promoting precarity digital labor platforms and the danish welfare state","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86676196/4S_Toronto_abstract.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381702","ids":["itu-1d0cc0d4-54ce-4dc3-bfe7-44c2aa8ce10e"],"keyword":["Algorithmic management","Digital welfare state","Gig economy","Platform labor","Precarious employment"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E66E46B29999022121B1AED9F74AB8C0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86676196/4S_Toronto_abstract.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Konstantinos","key":"florospau","last":"Floros","locid":"20ad2ae0-df32-484d-84ed-f65810600cfa","name":"Floros, Konstantinos","orcid":"0009-0002-4687-796X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-1d0cc0d4-54ce-4dc3-bfe7-44c2aa8ce10e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Fostering Flexibility or Promoting Precarity? : Digital Labor Platforms and the Danish Welfare State","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381702","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Because those who love never know what they love Or why they love, or what love is. To love is eternal innocence, And the only innocence is not to think… Fernando Pessoa wrote his Sheep Keeper long before the emergence of digital solutions, the fourth industrial revolution and the rise of surveillance capitalism. Let’s trade “love” with “consume” in the above verse and then let’s focus on digital labor platforms providing housecleaning. Danish citizens consuming platform mediated housecleaning services choose, book and pay their cleaners online in a seemingly seamless fashion. A few clicks suffice, if in a hurry they can even have the platform choose for them. What is the reality beneath these solutions, socially obscured by the eternal innocence of not thinking where the frictions lie? Beyond the amount of work put in by platform developers, administrators and users, the invisible added effort is on the workers’ side: They don’t only have to clean but also set up and update profiles, calculate their own hourly rates based on guesstimates, assume the risks of cancellations, injuries and sickness, put effort in creating their own companies, navigate complex tax issues, strive for good ratings from customers who are frequently very demanding and endure pressure from platforms’ algorithms and administrators for constant availability and customer satisfaction. Welfare digitalization adds to these frictions, since self-service solutions are more complex for the predominantly migrant population working through digital platforms. The (de)regulation of this segment of the labor market, brought about by local and European policies creates the adequate political ecology for the proliferation of platform work within an environment of structured uncertainty. In Denmark, the recent classification of platform workers as independent companies is a good example of that. However, if consumers’ strive for minimum effort explains their “innocent” blindness regarding unfair working conditions, the sustainment of such working conditions through state policies needs to be highlighted and explained more meticulously.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Floros, Konstantinos"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382292"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Platform economy","Platforms","Policy analysis"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"usynliggjorte friktioner og problemfri løsninger digitale serviceplatforme i danmark","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/portalfiles/portal/69699372/digital_gnidningsloeshed_og_arbejdet_bag_0.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382292","ids":["itu-44f0985f-9d2b-41a1-9b36-6ebc076a7385"],"keyword":["Policy analysis","platform economy","platforms"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-627FB440EF2C8A9015AD3C797176CCFE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/portalfiles/portal/69699372/digital_gnidningsloeshed_og_arbejdet_bag_0.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Konstantinos","key":"florospau","last":"Floros","locid":"20ad2ae0-df32-484d-84ed-f65810600cfa","name":"Floros, Konstantinos","orcid":"0009-0002-4687-796X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-44f0985f-9d2b-41a1-9b36-6ebc076a7385"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Usynliggjorte friktioner og problemfri løsninger : Digitale serviceplatforme i Danmark","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382292","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Algorithmic management of digital labor platforms and the ways in which they circumvent local labor law have produced a growing literature on the “gig economy” in recent years. Despite much attention paid to ridehailing and crowdwork platforms, research work on, the less tech-intensive, housecleaning platforms is limited. In August 2020, the Danish Competition and Consumer Authority published two decisions, which practically eliminated the opportunity of setting a minimum hourly fee for housecleaners contracting work through digital platforms. The Danish Authority thus deliberated on an issue which had not previously been clarified through legal procedures; that is the classification of platform workers as independent companies (self-employed persons). This decision highlights how modern digitalized states institutionalize precarity when regulating the digital environment of platform work. Moreover, the decision was issued without any consultation with the workers, which could reveal how ratings, surveillance and algorithmic management challenge the allegedly independent nature of such employment relations. This paper draws on empirical research, specifically policy document analysis and interviews conducted with platform workers and owners, politicians and public sector officials. It aims to critically engage with the role of Danish public authorities and policies in shaping platform work and think with the concept of governmental precarization (Lorey 2015), namely the normalization of insecurity for platform workers expected to self-govern their own precariousness. The proliferation of female, young, migrant -predominantly Latin-American- workers further problematizes the origins of such policymaking and the inequalities it promotes (cf. van Doorn et al. 2020). van Doorn, N., Ferrari, F., & Graham, M. (2020). Migration and Migrant Labour in the Gig Economy: An Intervention. SSRN Electronic Journal. https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.3622589 Lorey, I. (2015). State of insecurity: Government of the precarious. Verso Books.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Floros, Konstantinos"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384608"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Algorithmic management","Digital labor platforms","Governmental Precarization","Housecleaning Platforms","Platform Work Policies"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"when public authorities promote social dumping","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86676191/Konstantinos_Floros_Abstract_When_public_authorities_promote_social_dumping.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384608","ids":["itu-e29e5159-0977-4b45-a878-7bed0f4c973a"],"keyword":["Algorithmic Management","Digital Labor Platforms","Governmental Precarization","Housecleaning Platforms","Platform Work Policies"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-CFDD743BEBD1EC9386CEC14BD9D4BA6E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86676191/Konstantinos_Floros_Abstract_When_public_authorities_promote_social_dumping.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Konstantinos","key":"florospau","last":"Floros","locid":"20ad2ae0-df32-484d-84ed-f65810600cfa","name":"Floros, Konstantinos","orcid":"0009-0002-4687-796X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e29e5159-0977-4b45-a878-7bed0f4c973a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"When public authorities promote social dumping","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384608","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"This chapter examines the precarious and unfree labour conditions and im/mobility of Bangladeshi migrants in Manolada as well as in other agricultural geographies of their temporary relocation within Greece. Drawing upon a theoretical framework inspired by scholarship on migrants’ precarity, labour geography and the aspiration/capability model, the chapter explores im/mobility patterns as an outcome of both socio-legal constraints and migrants’ livelihood strategies. Based on 29 semi-structured on-site interviews with Bangladeshi migrants working in the strawberry-producing area of Manolada, we argue on the existence of a ‘mobility in immobility’ pattern, which is the outcome of migrants’ ‘state of expectancy’ regarding their regularization process and further mobility planning. We conclude on the importance of this pattern in shaping exclusionary geographies of coercion and unfreedom.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Kapsalis, Apostolos","Floros, Konstantinos","Jørgensen, Martin Bak"],"cuid":["S02-092.44-002725"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Greece"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Department of Social Policy, Panteion University & Department of Educational Policy, University of the Peloponnese","General Confederation of Greek Workers"],"keyword":["Agriculture","Manolada","Migration","Mobility","coercive geographies","state of expectancy"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"migrants entrapment in a state of expectancy patterns of im mobility for agricultural workers in manolada greece","src":["aau","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/92937968/Kapsalis_et_al._2020_Migrants_entrapment_in_a_state_of_expectancy_preprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-092.44-002725","ids":["aau-36e04613-43a7-4295-b1b0-eb0562c4a407","itu-e2a833a0-7f5a-469b-9a39-00e7112122be"],"keyword":["Agriculture","Manolada","coercive geographies","migration","mobility","state of expectancy"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-99D2710C03950484DABD8D616C2E6F00"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/92937968/Kapsalis_et_al._2020_Migrants_entrapment_in_a_state_of_expectancy_preprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gr","key":"general confederation of greek workers","loc":["Greece","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"General Confederation of Greek Workers","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gr","key":"department of social policy panteion university department of educational policy university of the peloponnese","loc":["Greece","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Department of Social Policy, Panteion University & Department of Educational Policy, University of the Peloponnese","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Culture and Learning","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Culture and Learning, Democracy, Migration and Society (DEMOS)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Apostolos","key":"kapsalispau","last":"Kapsalis","name":"Kapsalis, Apostolos","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Konstantinos","key":"florospau","last":"Floros","locid":"20ad2ae0-df32-484d-84ed-f65810600cfa","name":"Floros, Konstantinos","orcid":"0009-0002-4687-796X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Martin Bak","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"5e41f079-bbd2-4a57-a1d3-8fad8c9233de","name":"Jørgensen, Martin Bak","orcid":"0000-0003-0104-8543","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Johan","key":"heinsenpedh","last":"Heinsen","name":"Heinsen, Johan","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Martin","key":"bakjørgensenpedh","last":"Bak Jørgensen","name":"Bak Jørgensen, Martin","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Martin","key":"ottovayjørgensenpedh","last":"Ottovay Jørgensen","name":"Ottovay Jørgensen, Martin","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1163/9789004443204_003","eisbn":["9789004443204"],"isbn":["9789004443198"],"issn":["15734234"],"pages":"20-41","place":"Leiden","publisher":"Brill Academic Publishers","series":"Studies in Critical Social Sciences","status":"p","subtitle":"Historicizing Mobility, Labor and Confinement","title":"Coercive Geographies"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-36e04613-43a7-4295-b1b0-eb0562c4a407","itu-e2a833a0-7f5a-469b-9a39-00e7112122be"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Migrants’ Entrapment in a ‘State of Expectancy’ : Patterns of Im/mobility for Agricultural Workers in Manolada, Greece","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-092.44-002725","year":"2020","yearsub":2021}
{"abstract":"Restrictive migration laws, fracturing of citizenship and neoliberal labour markets intertwine with persistent migration flows to produce migrant precarity in the European context. This article examines the institutionalisation of precarious and unfree labour conditions for migrants in Denmark and Greece, through the enactment of laws and policy initiatives. The article situates itself in a literature regarding migrant precarity and its institutionalisation, unfree and informal labour and the production of immobility, which points to their interrelation as a constitutive element of modern European economies. In both cases, we can identify a retrenchment of rights and likewise the cases indicate that a fractured citizenship is instrumentalised in producing various types of immobility. The article concludes that despite differences between the European North and South we can identify a situation of unfree labour characterised by a lack of real or acceptable alternatives, within a setting of coercive geographies.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Floros, Konstantinos","Jørgensen, Martin Bak"],"cuid":["S02-093.18-004606"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Denmark","Greece","Labour","Migration","coercive geographies","institutionalisation of precarity"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"tracing the future of migrants labour relations experiences of institutionalised migrant precarity in denmark and greece","src":["aau","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.polgeo.2019.102120","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-093.18-004606","identifier":[{"id":"85075452043","type":"scopus"},{"id":"85073827920","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-5ee4b1a0-f60b-4972-b178-6499eedbead0","itu-f23c605c-c0c4-42a9-882f-85963e64b545"],"keyword":["Denmark","Greece","coercive geographies","institutionalisation of precarity","labour","migration"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-6C66B2DD5D5A6B97429D82D8116CA844"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.polgeo.2019.102120","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84873868/Floros_J_rgensen_PolGeo_post_print.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Culture and Learning","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Culture and Learning, Democracy, Migration and Society (DEMOS)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Konstantinos","key":"florospau","last":"Floros","locid":"20ad2ae0-df32-484d-84ed-f65810600cfa","name":"Floros, Konstantinos","orcid":"0009-0002-4687-796X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Martin Bak","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"5e41f079-bbd2-4a57-a1d3-8fad8c9233de","name":"Jørgensen, Martin Bak","orcid":"0000-0003-0104-8543","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.polgeo.2019.102120","issn":["09626298","18735096"],"issn_org":["09626298"],"journal":"Political Geography","paperid":"102120","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Political Geography","volume":"77"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85075452043"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-5ee4b1a0-f60b-4972-b178-6499eedbead0","itu-f23c605c-c0c4-42a9-882f-85963e64b545"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Tracing the future of migrants’ labour relations. Experiences of institutionalised migrant precarity in Denmark and Greece","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-093.18-004606","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
